# WolkaraForever layer patch 0077 - a replaced view layer takes the old one's place
#
# Why: NSView's setLayer: only stored the new layer. When the game replaces its content view's backing
# layer while the view is on screen (it rebuilds its renderer after the GPU device is lost, for example
# after a screen lock: the game logs "GxRestart. Reason:DeviceRecovery" and creates a new CAMetalLayer),
# the old layer stayed in the host's layer tree and the new one was in no tree at all. The game kept
# rendering complete frames into drawables that never reached the display: the picture froze on the old
# layer while the game ran. On an iPad Pro 11-inch (M5) this froze the world after a screen lock.
#
# setLayer: now puts the new layer where the old one was (same superlayer, same index, so overlays the
# host added above the game stay above it), moves the view's sublayers' layers onto it, gives a content
# view's layer the window's content scale, and removes the old layer. A layer detached by nil remembers
# its place for the next one. A replacement on screen logs "view layer replaced on screen: <Old> ->
# <New> (main thread=1|0)" once per replacement; an unhosted tree is reordered silently.
#
# Applies after: 0075-signing-enforcement-tests-skippable-where-not-enforced.patch
# Applies to: translation/AppKit/AppKit.m
# Upstream: yes (platform AppKit layer replacement bug).
#
